"The address to the Prophet at the beginning of the surah serves as a reminder and an indication of the importance of conveying the rulings in these verses to the ummah. The ruling of divorce is not exclusive to the Prophet, but is for all Muslims. Allah Almighty says: when you divorce women, divorce them taking into account their waiting period (iddah). That is, calculating the number of days of the woman's menstrual cycle, divorcing her during a period of purity without having had sexual intercourse, by a single pronouncement of divorce (talaq), is the manner conforming to the Sunnah.\n\nSince it is commanded to keep away from women during menstruation, divorcing during menstrual days is forbidden (haram). Likewise, divorcing during a period of purity after having had sexual intercourse is disliked (makruh). Issuing three divorces all at once is even more abhorrent and sinful. Such forms of divorce are innovations (bid'ah); while the divorce is valid, the person who executes it incurs sin. For divorce is a right entrusted to the will of men, and even though the act is sinful, the divorce takes effect.\n\nThe purpose of marriage is to establish a family based on the love and mercy that Allah has created between husband and wife. Even if there are discords or resentments, as long as natural desire and peace continue, the family should not be destroyed. However, if desire and tranquility cease and malice and hatred emerge, then divorce becomes a legitimate necessity. On the other hand, a divorce carried out in anger or out of lustful indulgence is ingratitude and often brings about regret.\n\nAlthough divorce has been made legitimate, there are manners of it that conform to the Sunnah. The best of these is to divorce the woman with a single pronouncement of divorce during a period of purity without having had sexual intercourse. This is called ahsan (the best). Issuing one divorce in each period of purity until reaching three divorces is considered hasan (good). In contrast, divorcing during menstruation or after sexual intercourse is an innovative divorce (talaq al-bid'ah); issuing three divorces at once is the severest innovation.\n\nThe expression 'divorce them taking into account their waiting period' mentioned in the verse was interpreted by Companions such as Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Abbas as 'while in a state of purity, before intercourse' or 'at the start of their waiting period.' Mujahid, Qatadah, and other scholars also supported this view. According to the Hanafis, the waiting period consists of the days of menstruation, whereas according to the Malikis and Shafi'is, it is the period of purity. However, all of them agreed that the woman must be divorced at a time when she is in a state of purity and no sexual intercourse has taken place.\n\nThe right of divorce belongs to the man. The woman does not have the direct authority to divorce; however, if this authority is granted by the man during or after the marriage contract, she may exercise it. Otherwise, the woman may seek separation through khul' (by offering a ransom). According to the Hanafis, the judge seeks reconciliation by appointing arbiters from the families of both parties; according to the Shafi'is, the judge has the right of annulment (faskh). The Hanafis accept annulment only in cases where the husband is incapable of sexual intercourse or in the event of illnesses such as leprosy.\n\nIt is forbidden to expel divorced women from their homes during their waiting periods. In the verse, it is stated: 'Do not expel them from their houses, nor should they leave.' This prohibition prevents the husband from casting the woman out due to anger or need, and it also encompasses the woman leaving on her own accord. However, if the woman commits open indecency and immorality, she may then be expelled from the house. This exception includes major sins such as adultery (zina), theft, and causing harm to family members. Most scholars have stated that 'fahishah' refers to any major sin.\n\nFearing Allah means observing the commands and prohibitions concerning divorce and the waiting period. It is not permissible to undercount or artificially prolong the waiting period. Harming women must be avoided. Acting in accordance with the Sunnah during divorce prevents regret and delays the dissolution of the family. It must not be forgotten that divorce is among the most detestable of permissible things in the sight of Allah, and it should only be resorted to in cases of necessity.\n\nThese verses are the limits set by Allah for His servants. Whoever exceeds these limits has wronged himself. Perhaps Allah will bring about a new situation thereafter, and when one regrets and wishes to return, it will be too late. For this reason, in the process of divorce, it is necessary to observe the waiting period, pronounce divorce during a state of purity, and count the waiting period. As long as it does not reach three, the divorce is revocable (raj'i); that is, reconciliation is possible during the waiting period. Because of these wisdoms, do not exceed the limits, count the waiting period, and fear Allah; do not expel women from their houses, nor should they leave.\n\nWhen the waiting period reaches its end, either retain them with kindness or separate from them with kindness. Retention can also take place through an act; this is the view of the Great Imam (Abu Hanifah). When separating, release them gracefully and call to witness two just men. The command to bring witnesses has generally been understood as recommended (mandub). Although al-Shafi'i argued in his earlier view that it was obligatory, in his later view he stated it was recommended. Al-Alusi finds Tabarsi's view—that this command makes bringing witnesses obligatory at the moment of divorce—to be weak. The apparent meaning of the verse requires witnessing to take place at the time of retention or separation; the claim that it is a condition for the validity of divorce is far-fetched. This is because such a condition would contradict the benefit provided by revocable divorce and the Sunnah manner of divorce. Moreover, a contradiction would arise where a divorce would be invalid if witnesses were not present during the divorce but were brought afterward. For this reason, all mujtahids are in agreement that the command to bring witnesses upon separation is recommended. Then, uphold the testimony uprightly for Allah. All these commands are an admonition to those who believe in Allah and the Last Day; they are the ones who benefit.\n\nWhoever fears Allah, Allah creates a way out for him and provides for him from where he does not expect. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recited this verse and said: 'Allah creates a way out from the doubts of this world, the agony of death, and the severity of the Day of Resurrection.' Narrated from Abu Dharr: 'If all people held fast to this, it would suffice them.' Whoever relies upon Allah, He is sufficient for him. Allah accomplishes His purpose; He has destined a measure for all things. Relying on worldly causes is finite, while relying on Allah is infinite. Reliance upon Allah (tawakkul) means performing one's duty to the best of one's ability and leaving the outcome to Allah. First piety (taqwa), then reliance is commanded. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Tie your camel and put your trust in Allah.' Just as Allah has set a measure for everything, He has also set limits for the waiting period, maintenance, and other matters.\n\nThe waiting period of women who have passed menopause is three months. Those who have not menstruated also wait three months. This includes those under seventeen years of age who have not yet reached puberty, or those who have reached puberty but do not menstruate. This ruling also applies to those who menstruated once or twice and then stopped. In the case of continuous cessation of menstruation, it is said that 'the period of purity has lengthened'; the Hanafis state that such a woman must wait until the age of menopause. However, since the primary purpose of the waiting period is to eliminate any doubt of pregnancy, waiting up to the maximum duration of pregnancy appears more appropriate in such cases. As for pregnant women, their waiting period ends when they deliver their burden. This applies both in the case of divorce and upon the husband's death. Although Ali and Ibn Abbas initially thought this verse applied only to divorced women and accepted the longer of the two terms (four months and ten days or childbirth), the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) became known through the hadith of Umm Salamah: 'When you have given birth, you have become lawful.' This hadith, authentically narrated in al-Bukhari and other sources, indicates that Surah at-Talaq was revealed after the verse concerning the mourning waiting period upon death in Surah al-Baqarah, and that it abrogated the ruling regarding pregnant women.\n\nThese rulings are the command of Allah. Whoever fears Allah, Allah will expiate his sins and magnify his reward. In the surah, recommendations of piety (taqwa) are repeated, drawing attention to the importance of family law and the dangers of carelessness regarding divorce. Divorce is the most hateful of permissible things (halal) in the sight of Allah. For this reason, when divorce is necessary, it is commanded to observe the waiting period (iddah), not to expel them from their homes, and then either to retain them with kindness or separate from them with kindness.\n\nLodge the divorced women where you live according to your means, and do not harass them in order to distress them. If they are pregnant, spend on them until they deliver. If they breastfeed for you, give them their payment and consult among yourselves in a fair manner. If you encounter difficulties, another woman may breastfeed for him. Let the wealthy person spend according to his abundance, and let the one whose provision is restricted spend from what Allah has granted him. Allah does not burden any soul beyond what He has given it. Allah will bring about ease after hardship.\n\nIn matters pertaining to women such as menstruation and pregnancy, and in circumstances like the cessation of menstruation or pregnancy, the woman's statement is taken as the basis. As stated in verse 228 of Surah al-Baqarah, it is not lawful for women who believe in Allah and the Last Day to conceal what Allah has created in their wombs. The waiting period (iddah) of a divorced woman is to determine whether she is pregnant; if it becomes known that she is pregnant, she does not need to wait for three menstrual periods, but waits until she gives birth, and maintenance (nafaqah) is provided during this time. The duration of pregnancy is at least six months, and at most two years according to the Hanafis and four years according to the Shafi'is. The condition 'if they are pregnant' here is not the reason for the command of maintenance, but rather the justification for spending throughout the period leading up to childbirth. This condition does not mean that non-pregnant divorced women will not receive maintenance; on the contrary, it indicates that spending is obligatory (wajib) even during short waiting periods.\n\nThe command in the verse 'Do not expel them, lodge them' indirectly conveys that spending (infaq) is also one of the requisites of lodging, alongside the prohibition of expelling them from the home. There are several points of wisdom in specifying the condition of pregnancy: First, to show that the primary purpose of the waiting period and maintenance is pregnancy. Second, to dispel doubt against the possibility of a prolonged pregnancy. Third, it points to the need for showing special care toward pregnant women. Fourth, to explain the rulings regarding the breastfeeding and care of the child after birth. After the child is born, if the mother breastfeeds on the father's account, the father must pay the breastfeeding fee. If the mother breastfeeds on her own account, the father covers only clothing and other expenses. In both cases, it is necessary to reach an agreement through kind and proper mutual consultation. If a dispute arises, the father is obliged to hire another wet nurse. However, if the child refuses the breast of another woman, the mother may be compelled to breastfeed by a judicial arbitrator's ruling.\n\nIn the 7th verse, it is commanded that the one of abundance should spend according to his abundance, and the one whose provision is restricted should spend from what Allah has granted him. This is a ruling applicable to all maintenance matters. The person obligated to spend is responsible according to his wealth if he is rich, according to his modest means if he is middle-class, and according to his poverty if he is poor. Allah does not burden anyone beyond his capability. One who can borrow may spend by borrowing in good faith. Allah will bring about ease after hardship. For this reason, the poor and their families should be patient and never despair of Allah, and the wealthy should not neglect them but look after them through zakat and charity (sadaqah).\n\nIn verses 8-10, it is reported that many a city that rebelled against the command of Allah and His messengers was called to a severe account and subjected to an unprecedented punishment. These societies tasted the consequence of their deeds, and the outcome of their affair was ruin. Allah has prepared a severe punishment for them. O people of understanding who have believed! Fear Allah, for Allah has sent down to you a reminder (the Quran or a messenger).\n\nIn the 11th verse, it is stated that Allah sent a messenger reciting the clear verses of Allah to bring those who believe and do righteous deeds out of darkness into light. Whoever believes in Allah and does righteous deeds, Allah will admit him into gardens beneath which rivers flow, to abide therein forever. Allah has indeed granted him an excellent provision. What is meant by the messenger is our Prophet Muhammad Mustafa (peace be upon him). The tanwin (nunation) in the word 'Rasul' expresses grandeur.\n\nIn the 12th verse, it is reported that Allah created seven heavens and of the earth a similar number, and that the command descends between them. This is so that we may know that Allah is capable of all things and that His knowledge encompasses everything. While the expression 'seven heavens' is definite, it does not mean there is nothing beyond them. The contrary implication of numbers (mafhum al-mukhalafah) is not valid unless there is corroborating evidence. Ayat al-Kursi and the hadiths show that the Kursi (Footstool) is vaster than the heavens and is like a ring within the Throne (Arsh). The seven heavens may also be understood as regions demarcated by the orbits of the seven known planets; however, this number does not preclude the existence of other planets.\n\nThe similarity in the phrase 'and of the earth, their like' pertains to number and certain attributes. According to the majority of commentators, just like the seven heavens, there are seven earths, and between each of them there is a distance equivalent to that between heaven and earth. In each earth, there are living creatures created by Allah. Some commentators, however, stated that it refers to the strata of the earth or the seven climes. Sound hadiths also support the existence of seven earths. The wisdom behind the Quran usually mentioning 'the heavens and the earth' in other verses and using the singular 'of the earth' here is that the earth serves as a single gravitational center, whereas the sky, as an expanse of ascent, is manifold. This demonstrates that Allah's knowledge and power encompass all things.\n\nIf the 'lam' (definite article) in the expression 'al-Ard' in the verse denotes the genus (jins), it is understood as a resemblance of the terrestrial genus to the seven heavens—meaning that planets, like the earth, are centers of gravity and habitations. If the 'lam' denotes specific reference (ahd), it indicates a resemblance to the seven heavens solely in terms of the continents, strata, and regions of our own earth. All of these interpretations may be correct, and each offers a distinct perspective in understanding Allah's power.\n\nHere, it is noted that the word \"in\" may have two different meanings. One of these is that the word is an \"ibtidaiyyah\" particle, that is, a preposition denoting inception. In this case, the verse describes the creation of human beings from dust and their subsequent coming into existence with the command \"Be\". Indeed, similar expressions are found in verses Al-i Imran 3/59 and Ta-Ha 20/55. This interpretation encompasses the creation of the human being in both body and soul.\n\nAccording to the second interpretation, what is meant is the descent of divine commands between the seven layers of heaven and the earth. As stated in Fussilat 41/12, He revealed its command to each heaven. These commands regulate the movement and rest, as well as the events and affairs, of all beings. In this way, divine revelation regarding divine decree and destiny (al-qada' wa'l-qadar) descends continuously.\n\nThe point to be noted here is that all events in systems of celestial bodies, such as the solar system, do not occur by their own nature, but by the command of Allah. As expressed in al-Isra 17/85, the soul (ruh) is also from the command of the Lord. In the same way, the Quran was also sent down into the heart of our Prophet as a light descending from above the heavens.\n\nAll these acts of creation and command are so that we may know that Allah has power over all things. He is also fully capable of fulfilling what He has promised to those who believe and perform righteous deeds. Moreover, Allah's knowledge encompasses all things; the creation and order of the universe stand as proof of His power and knowledge.\n\nSurah at-Talaq ends here. In connection with the subject of prohibition, which is one of the consequences of divorce (talaq), Surah at-Tahrim will follow next. This surah also pertains to oaths and family matters."